ution queries (starter-list GCN papers are not supported by the run's methods)\",\n497\t        \"any query targeting the specific published paper on this research question, its title, authors, or results\",\n498\t        \"internal harness terms (combined_score, rubric, eligible, worker)\"\n499\t      ],\n500\t      \"queries\": [\n501\t        {\n502\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n503\t          \"query\": \"regression adjustment global average treatment effect network interference\",\n504\t          \"rationale\": \"Central estimator family (Chin 2019 and follow-ups); needed to position the extrapolation-error characterization.\"\n505\t        },\n506\t        {\n507\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n508\t          \"query\": \"model-based regression adjustment network interference graph covariates bootstrap confidence intervals\",\n509\t          \"rationale\": \"Closest method family (Han & Ugander) and source of block-bootstrap inference comparable to our design-resampling bootstrap.\"\n510\t        },\n511\t        {\n512\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n513\t          \"query\": \"exposure mapping misspecification bias network interference total treatment effect estimation\",\n514\t          \"rationale\": \"Run shows bias dominates when the functional form of interference is wrong; need literature on misspecified exposure mappings.\"\n515\t        },\n516\t        {\n517\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n518\t          \"query\": \"Horvitz-Thompson Hajek estimator general interference exposure probability variance\",\n519\t          \"rationale\": \"HT and Hajek baselines were run; Aronow & Samii and variance-bound work are expected comparators.\"\n520\t        },\n521\t        {\n522\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n523\t          \"query\": \"graph cluster randomization network exposure bias variance tradeoff\",\n524\t          \"rationale\": \"Design-side comparator for reducing interference bias; relevant to the design axis of the simulation.\"\n525\t        },\n526\t        {\n527\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n528\t          \"query\": \"difference-in-means bias under interference randomized experiments SUTVA violation\",\n529\t          \"rationale\": \"Naive difference-in-means baseline had rmse_rel ~0.62 versus 0.14-0.18 for regression estimators.\"\n530\t        },\n531\t        {\n532\t          \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n533\t          \"query\": \"augmented inverse probability weighting doubly robust residual correction interference network\",\n534\t          \"rationale\": \"Second variant adds design-weighted residual correction to a linear base, an AIPW/doubly-robust-style construction.\"\n535\t        },\n536\t        {\n537\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n538\t          \"query\": \"staggered rollout design total treatment effect interference without network knowledge\",\n539\t          \"rationale\": \"Alternative design/estimator family for GATE under low-order interference, named in starter literature.\"\n540\t        },\n541\t        {\n542\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n543\t          \"query\": \"extrapolation all-treated all-control counterfactual positivity violation network exposure\",\n544\t          \"rationale\": \"Central finding: error governed by extrapolation to rarely observed full-exposure corner under random designs.\"\n545\t        },\n546\t        {\n547\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n548\t          \"query\": \"Facebook100 social structure of Facebook networks\",\n549\t          \"rationale\": \"Public graph data context if Facebook100 graphs were used for evaluation; cite data provenance only.\"\n550\t        }\n551\t      ],\n552\t      \"result_driven_themes\": [\n553\t        \"extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corner makes bias dominate error (bias_rel ~0.16-0.19 vs rmse ~0.18-0.23) for flexible models\",\n554\t        \"correctly specified exposure functional form reduces error in nonlinear-interference cases (case 6: 0.71 -> 0.27) while some cases remain unresolved (case 8 ~0.45)\",\n555\t        \"bootstrap CI undercoverage (0.67-0.81) under misspecification\",\n556\t        \"model selection by information criterion for exposure-response specification\"\n557\t      ],\n558\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n559\t    },\n560\t    {\n561\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n562\t        \"flexible regression ensemble (worse than linear) -> linear base with shrunk design-weighted residual correction -> BIC-gated parametric exposure-mapping family with linear fallback\"\n563\t      ],\n564\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n565\t        \"synthetic networks (SBM, Barabasi-Albert, Watts-Strogatz) with synthetic interference outcomes\",\n566\t        \"Facebook100 / socfb-Stanford3 and SNAP ego-Facebook public graphs only if used by the run\"\n567\t      ],\n568\t      \"central_topic\": \"Estimation error of regression-based global average treatment effect (GATE) estimators under network interference, characterized as corner-exposure extrapolation bias versus variance, across interference mechanisms, graphs and designs.\",\n569\t      \"comparators\": [\n570\t        \"difference-in-means (naive SUTVA) estimator\",\n571\t        \"Horvitz-Thompson / Hajek estimators under exposure mappings (Aronow & Samii)\",\n572\t        \"graph cluster randomization and network exposure (Ugander et al.; Eckles et al.)\",\n573\t        \"model-based regression adjustment with graph-based covariates (Han & Ugander)\",\n574\t        \"staggered rollout and covariance-optimized designs for total t\n575\t\n576\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n577\t```\n578\t\n579\t## manuscript source excerpt\n580\t\n581\t```tex\n582\t\\documentclass{article}\n583\t\n584\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n585\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n586\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n587\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n588\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n589\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n590\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n591\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n592\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n593\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n594\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n595\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n596\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n597\t}\n598\t\n599\t \n600\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n601\t\n602\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n603\t\\usepackage{url}\n604\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n605\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n606\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n607\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n608\t\n609\t\\title{How Accurate Is Regression-Based Global Effect Estimation Under Network Interference? An Eight-Case Synthetic Evaluation}\n610\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n611\t\n612\t\\newcommand{\\gate}{\\mathrm{GATE}}\n613\t\n614\t\\begin{document}\n615\t\\maketitle\n616\t\n617\t\\begin{abstract}\n618\tIn online experiments on social networks and marketplaces, a user's outcome depends on other users' treatments, and the quantity of interest is the global average treatment effect (GATE): the mean outcome difference between treating everyone and treating no one. Regression-based estimators fit an outcome model on exposure features from one randomized experiment and difference its predictions at the all-treated and all-control corners, but how accurate they are, and what determines their error, is poorly characterized. We measure this on an eight-case synthetic grid (3000 nodes; stochastic-block, Barab\\'asi--Albert and Watts--Strogatz graphs; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), scored on 24 unseen seeds. A frozen estimator that selects among 15 low-parameter exposure mappings with a BIC gate reaches a mean relative RMSE of 0.126, against 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, 0.622 for difference-in-means, 0.994 for a neighbourhood Horvitz--Thompson estimator, and 0.045 for an oracle regression given the true mapping. Per-case error is 0.031--0.054 in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, across three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs, and 0.151--0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping misspecification and corner extrapolation drive the larger errors, but the grid is not factorial and the estimator's per-case mapping choices were not verified, so this account is untested. Nominal 95\\% intervals cover only 0.839 of the time. The evidence is limited to one graph size, case specifications shared between model selection and final scoring, and single-factor contrasts without per-case intervals.\n619\t\\end{abstract}\n620\t\n621\t\\section{Introduction}\n622\tA/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased for the GATE under interference \\citep{Eckles2016Design,Ugander2013GraphCluster}, and design-based alternatives such as Horvitz--Thompson (HT) estimators of neighbourhood exposure \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Ugander2013GraphCluster} avoid modelling assumptions at the price of large variance when the all-treated and all-control neighbourhood events are rare.\n623\t\n624\tA model-based alternative is \\emph{regression-based GATE estimation} \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF}: fit a regression of outcomes on the unit's own treatment, a summary of its neighbours' treatments (an \\emph{exposure mapping}) and covariates; predict every unit's outcome under the all-treated and all-control assignments; and average the difference. This is attractive because it uses every unit rather than only those with extreme neighbourhoods. Its weakness is also visible in the construction: the two prediction points are the corners of exposure space (neighbour-treated fraction 1 and 0), which a randomized experiment with a moderate treatment probability rarely visits, so the estimate leans on the functional form of the fitted model. How accurate such estimators are in practice, which features of the mechanism, graph and design drive their error, and when they fail has not been laid out in a controlled grid.\n625\t\n626\t\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? The measured pattern is that per-case error is low in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction and higher in the three cases whose form is not, with no visible degradation across graph family, interference strength (up to three times larger) or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals undercover. A natural hypothesis, which the interference literature raises under the heading of misspecified exposure mappings and interference of unknown form \\citep{Savje2023Misspecified,Savje2021Unknown}, is that accuracy depends on how well the fitted exposure mapping matches the generating mechanism. We state this as a hypothesis: the grid is not factorial, and the mapping the estimator selected in each case was not recorded in verified form.\n627\t\n628\t\\paragraph{What we do.} We evaluate one frozen estimator, a BIC-gated library of parametric exposure mappings with linear regression as the fallback, together with four reference estimators (difference-in-means, plain linear exposure regression, a neighbourhood HT estimator and an oracle regression using the true mapping) on a fixed eight-case grid, using 24 seeds that were not used for model selection. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the pipeline and the grid.\n629\t\n630\t\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A transparent, scale-free protocol (relative RMSE per case, averaged over cases) with four reference estimators and three negative controls. (ii) Measured accuracy of regression-based GATE estimation across five interference forms, three graph families and two assignment designs, with single-factor case contrasts that are consistent with, but do not establish, exposure-mapping misspecification as the main error source. (iii) Negative results: nominal 95\\% intervals cover 0.839 of the time, the two-hop case stays at relative RMSE 0.452 and its cause is unresolved, and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) scored worse than the gated library on the model-selection seeds. We make no claim of a bound, a theorem, or superiority beyond this grid; Section~\\ref{sec:limits} lists what the evidence cannot support.\n631\t\n632\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n633\t\\centering\n634\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_schematic.png}\n635\t\\caption{Overview. A randomized assignment on a network yields exposure features (own treatment, neighbour-treated fraction or count, optional two-hop and degree terms); a fitted outcome regression is evaluated at the observed assignment and at the all-treated and all-control corners, and the average difference is the GATE estimate. The eight-case grid varies graph family, response shape, spillover range, strength and assignment design. Error can come from sampling noise or from misspecification of the exposure mapping, which matters most at the rarely observed corners.}\n636\t\\label{fig:overview}\n637\t\\end{figure}\n638\t\n639\t\\section{Related Work}\n640\t\\paragraph{Design-based estimation under interference.} Randomization-based frameworks define exposure conditions over a network and estimate their effects with inverse-probability weighting \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC}; graph cluster randomization raises the probability that a unit's whole neighbourhood is treated together \\citep{Ugander2013GraphCluster,Ugander2020RandomizedGC}, and it has been deployed at scale \\citep{Karrer2020NetworkEA}. Subsequent work studies estimation theory under network interference \\citep{Sussman2017Elements}, the admissibility of HT estimation \\citep{Karwa2023Admissibility}, data-adaptive exposure thresholds for HT \\citep{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}, interference with noisy or $k$-nearest-neighbour structure \\citep{Li2021Noise,Alzubaidi2023KNN}, and asymptotics for effect estimation under random-graph models of the network \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph}. Our HT reference is of this family; it is included to show how variable a design-based estimator is at this sample size, not as a tuned competitor.\n641\t\n642\t\\paragraph{Misspecified exposure mappings.} \\citet{Savje2023Misspecified} treats a misspecified analyst mapping and separates the estimand's definition from the assumptions that make an estimator informative about it; \\citet{Savje2021Unknown} study average-effect estimation under interference of unknown form. These works are analytical; we instead measure in a simulation grid how far a regression GATE estimator lands from the all-treated-minus-all-control contrast when the mechanism is or is not linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, and offer the corner-extrapolation account of Section~\\ref{sec:corner} as a heuristic.\n643\t\n644\t\\paragraph{Designs for the GATE.} Several works change the assignment mechanism instead of the analysis: clustered and cluster-adaptive designs \\citep{Viviano2023CausalClustering,Zhou2020ClusterAdaptive,Nandy2019Dense}, staggered or two-stage rollouts \\citep{Cortez2024TwoStage}, designs matched to low-order outcome models \\citep{Eichhorn2024LoworderOA}, optimized covariance of the assignment vector \\citep{Chen2023OptimizedCD}, multi-arm designs under partial interference \\citep{Offer-Westort2021DesigningME}, experimental design with network information \\citep{Viviano2020Experimental}, and mixtures of randomized experiments \\citep{Jiang2023Mixture}. We hold the two designs fixed (Bernoulli and block-cluster) and study analysis only.\n645\t\n646\t\\paragraph{Regression adjustment and model-based estimation.} \\citet{Chin2018RegressionAF} proposed predicting the all-treated and all-control worlds from a fitted outcome model and analyzed it under linear models, with a companion central limit theorem \\citep{Chin2018Stein}. \\citet{Han2023ModelbasedRA} select graph-based and treatment-based covariates for the regression and use block bootstrap intervals. Generalized propensity-score approaches model exposure probabilities \\citep{Forastiere2022EstimatingCE}, and \\citet{Shankar2024ABTU} and \\citet{Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty} address estimation when the network is only partly known. Outside networks, regression adjustment is a variance-reduction tool in randomized experiments \\citep{Wager2016HighdimensionalRA,Ting2023Limits}. Our contribution relative to this literature is empirical: a controlled grid that separates cases where the fitted mapping matches the mechanism from cases where it does not, with explicit reference estimators, negative controls and an interval-calibration check. We do not derive new theory.\n647\t\n648\t\\section{Method}\n649\t\\subsection{Problem setup}\n650\tA graph $G$ on $n=3000$ nodes has adjacency $A$ and degree $d_i$. Each unit has covariates $x_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ and a binary assignment $z_i$ drawn by the design. Outcomes follow $Y_i(\\mathbf z)=\\mu_i(\\mathbf z)+\\varepsilon_i$ with $\\varepsilon_i\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. The target is\n651\t\\begin{equation}\n652\t\\gate=\\frac1n\\sum_{i}\\big[\\mu_i(\\mathbf 1)-\\mu_i(\\mathbf 0)\\big].\n653\t\\end{equation}\n654\tAn estimator receives the graph, covariates, assignment, observed outcomes, the design description (kind, probability, cluster labels) and a nominal level of 0.95, and returns an estimate and an interval. It never sees the mechanism or the truth.\n655\t\n656\t\\paragraph{Metric.} The per-run error is relative, $e=\\min(|\\hat\\tau-\\gate|/|\\gate|,\\,2)$; instances are constructed so that $|\\gate|\\ge 0.5$. For each case the evaluator takes the RMSE of $e$ over seeds, and the plane score is the unweighted mean over the eight cases (``mean relative RMSE''; lower is better). We use this because GATE magnitudes differ across mechanisms and the cap of 2 keeps a high-variance estimator from dominating the average; a failed or invalid response counts as $e=2$. We also report the mean over cases of the absolute mean signed relative error (``bias''), the mean coverage of the 95\\% interval, and its mean width divided by $|\\gate|$ (capped at 4).\n657\t\n658\t\\subsection{Simulation grid}\n659\tTable~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the eight cases; full parameters are in Supplementary Table~\\ref{tab:spec}. The outcome mean is $\\mu_i(\\mathbf z)=1+x_i^\\top\\beta+(\\tau_0+\\tau_1x_{i1})z_i+h_i(\\mathbf z)$ with $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$ and an interference term $h_i$ built from the neighbour-treated fraction $f_i=\\sum_jA_{ij}z_j/\\max(d_i,1)$:\n660\t\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{0pt}\n661\t\\item \\emph{fraction}: $h_i=\\gamma f_i$ with $\\gamma=1.5$ (cases 1--4) or $\\gamma=4.5$ (case 5);\n662\t\\item \\emph{saturating}: $h_i=\\gamma/(1+e^{-10(f_i-0.5)})$ with $\\gamma=2.5$ (case 6);\n663\t\\item \\emph{count$\\times$degree}: $h_i=0.15\\,c_i\\sqrt{10/\\max(d_i,1)}$, $c_i=\\sum_jA_{ij}z_j$ (case 7);\n664\t\\item \\emph{two-hop}: $h_i=1.5f_i+2.0\\,f^{(2)}_i$, where $f^{(2)}_i$ is the treated fraction among nodes at distance exactly two (case 8).\n665\t\\end{itemize}\n666\tGraphs are a ten-block stochastic block model (SBM; expected within-block degree 14, between-block 6), a Barab\\'asi--Albert (BA) graph with $m=10$ and a Watts--Strogatz (WS) graph with $k=8$ and rewiring probability 0.1. Assignment is Bernoulli with $p=0.5$, or (case 2) five of the ten SBM blocks chosen uniformly and treated together. In SBM cases the first covariate carries a block-level shift. The graphs and truth are generated by an evaluator that is private to the estimator.\n667\t\n668\t\\begin{table}[t]\n669\t\\centering\\small\n670\t\\caption{Eight-case grid and frozen-estimator error on the 24 unseen seeds (relative RMSE per case). Each factor contrast in the text is a single case pair.}\n671\t\\label{tab:cases}\n672\t\\begin{tabular}{clllc}\n673\t\\toprule\n674\tCase & Graph & Interference form & Assignment & Rel.\\ RMSE\\\\\n675\t\\midrule\n676\t1 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.051\\\\ \n677\t2 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster & 0.031\\\\ \n678\t3 & BA & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.054\\\\ \n679\t4 & WS & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.040\\\\ \n680\t5 & SBM & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli & 0.034\\\\ \n681\t6 & SBM & saturating sigmoid & Bernoulli & 0.198\\\\ \n682\t7 & BA & count$\\times$degree & Bernoulli & 0.151\\\\ \n683\t8 & SBM & fraction + two-hop & Bernoulli & 0.452\\\\ \n684\t\\bottomrule\n685\t\\end{tabular}\n686\t\\end{table}\n687\t\n688\t\\subsection{Compared estimators}\n689\t\\paragraph{Plain linear exposure regression (baseline).} Ordinary least squares of $y$ on $[1,\\,z,\\,f,\\,x]$, predicting at $z=\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 0$ (with $f=1$ for non-isolated nodes at the all-treated corner and $f=0$ at the all-control corner). This is the canonical regression GATE estimator \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF} with a one-hop fraction mapping, as implemented by the evaluator (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py}, \\texttt{\\_linear\\_features} and \\texttt{\\_ols\\_gate}). Its mapping is linear in the neighbour fraction, as is the generating term in cases 1--5; the generating own-treatment effect also includes a treatment-by-covariate interaction that this regression omits.\n690\t\n691\t\\paragraph{Frozen BIC-gated exposure library (studied estimator).} The studied estimator (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, functions \\texttt{candidate\\_designs}, \\texttt{bic\\_gate}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}) fits by least squares the linear model above plus 14 alternatives, each with few parameters: a treated-neighbour count divided by the mean degree; a two-hop walk fraction; interactions with log-degree and covariates (three variants); saturating $1-e^{-kf}$ with $k\\in\\{1,3,8\\}$; sigmoids of $f$ centred at $\\{0.25,0.5,0.75\\}$ with scale 0.1; and saturating functions of the count scaled by $\\{0.5,1,2\\}\\times$ the median degree. Exposure features are computed analytically at the observed, all-treated and all-control assignments. In the count mapping only, the all-treated feature is capped at three times the 99.5th percentile of its observed values. An alternative is kept only if its BIC is lower than the linear model's by more than 10; kept alternatives are averaged with BIC weights, and if none passes, or the average departs from the linear estimate by more than three times $\\max(|\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|,\\mathrm{se}_{\\mathrm{lin}})$, the linear estimate is returned. The interval is $\\hat\\tau\\pm\\big(1.96\\,\\mathrm{se}+|\\hat\\tau-\\hat\\tau_{\\mathrm{lin}}|\\big)$, where $\\mathrm{se}$ is the BIC-weighted mean of HC1 sandwich delta-method standard errors plus the weighted spread of the kept estimates. The estimator is deterministic, reads neither the design description nor the cluster labels, and uses no resampling. It was written after inspecting model-selection results; its per-case gate decisions were not logged in verified form (the evaluation marks candidate diagnostics as unverified), so we cannot report which mapping it selected in any case. In particular, the library contains the exact sigmoid form used to generate case 6 (centre 0.5; steepness 10 corresponds to scale 0.1), so the case-6 error cannot be read as absence of the true form from the library.\n692\t\n693\t\\paragraph{Reference estimators (evaluator-owned).} \\emph{DiM}: treated mean minus control mean. \\emph{HT}: a neighbourhood-exposure Horvitz--Thompson estimator that keeps units whose closed neighbourhood is entirely treated (or entirely control) and weights by the design-implied probability of that event. \\emph{Oracle}: OLS with the true exposure mapping for the case plus covariates and an own-by-covariate term; it is a reference for correct specification, not an attainable method. Definitions follow \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py} (\\texttt{baseline\\_dim}, \\texttt{baseline\\_ht}, \\texttt{\\_oracle\\_features\\_for}).\n694\t\n695\t\\paragraph{Negative controls.} The evaluator also sends matched requests with permuted outcomes, a node-relabelled graph and a constant outcome, and replays one episode. They are intended to probe whether the estimate reacts to scrambled graph or outcome structure and whether the estimator is deterministic.\n696\t\n697\t\\subsection{Protocol and scope}\n698\tModel selection used eight seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications; three estimator classes were each scored once there (Supplementary Table~\\ref{tab:search}). One estimator was then frozen and evaluated once on 24 seeds (9001--9024) with fresh graphs and assignments, which is the evidence for all headline numbers. No invalid or failed runs occurred on the final plane. The final evaluation completed with outcome \\emph{qualified}; this means the evaluator's declared requirements were met on this grid and does not by itself establish validity, generality or superiority beyond it. Because case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, the final gain over the linear baseline measures behaviour on new seeds of known mechanism families, not transfer to new mechanisms.\n699\t\n700\t\\section{Results}\n701\t\\subsection{Accuracy and baselines}\n702\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n703\t\\centering\n704\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_accuracy.pdf}\n705\t\\caption{Final plane, 24 unseen seeds. (a) Mean relative RMSE over the eight cases for the oracle, the frozen estimator, linear exposure regression, difference-in-means (DiM) and neighbourhood HT (log axis). (b) Frozen-estimator relative RMSE per case (SBM: Bernoulli assignment; clus.: SBM with cluster assignment; str.: strong interference; sat.: saturating; c$\\times$d: count$\\times$degree; 2-hop: two-hop); dashed lines are the oracle and linear plane means. Per-case values for the reference estimators are not available.}\n706\t\\label{fig:main}\n707\t\\end{figure}\n708\tFigure~\\ref{fig:main}a gives the headline comparison. The frozen estimator attains a mean relative RMSE of 0.126 \n709\t versus 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression \n710\t(ratio 0.701) \n711\t and 0.622 for DiM \n712\t(ratio 0.203). \n713\t The neighbourhood HT estimator averages 0.994 \n714\t over the capped relative-error scale, which at $n=3000$ is not usable; we did not log the number of units with fully treated or fully control neighbourhoods, but the estimate is evidently dominated by noise or by the cap. The oracle regression reaches 0.045, \n715\t so the frozen estimator remains 2.8 times above the correct-specification reference. \n716\t The ordering oracle $<$ frozen $<$ linear $\\ll$ DiM $<$ HT is a plane-level statement: per-case values for the baselines were not recorded, so we cannot say in which cases the linear baseline loses to the frozen estimator.\n717\t\n718\tTable~\\ref{tab:agg} lists the aggregate metrics.\n719\t\n720\t\\begin{table}[t]\n721\t\\centering\\small\n722\t\\caption{Final-plane aggregate metrics (24 seeds $\\times$ 8 cases). Bias is the mean over cases of the absolute mean signed relative error; coverage and width are for the nominal 95\\% interval and are available only for the studied estimator.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e77e47a8-1973-482c-8d1e-3bed91288163","uuid":"8b9b0fcb-fa81-43d7-a75c-f866337feea5","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:49:05.612Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T08:49:01.843568+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t09/claude/20261111_071431/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261111_081253-paper_evidence_audit_round_2-46gy6ifo/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, and `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- every visible claim in Supplementary Information, including tables and figures, under the same grounding standard as the main paper\n\n## Artifact checklist\n\n- `paper.tex` — `present`\n- `claims_grounding.json` — `present`\n- `paper_data.json` — `present`\n- `validation_report.json` — `present`\n- `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` — `present`\n- `refe