{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SgBfTccAbqYpWGkSxjhYH5","type":"tool_result","content":"136\t\n137\tTable~\\ref{tab:agg} lists the aggregate metrics.\n138\t\n139\t\\begin{table}[t]\n140\t\\centering\\small\n141\t\\caption{Final-plane aggregate metrics (24 seeds $\\times$ 8 cases). Bias is the mean over cases of the absolute mean signed relative error; coverage and width are for the nominal 95\\% interval and are available only for the studied estimator.}\n142\t\\label{tab:agg}\n143\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n144\t\\toprule\n145\tEstimator & Rel.\\ RMSE & Ratio to linear & Bias & Coverage & Rel.\\ width\\\\\n146\t\\midrule\n147\tOracle (true mapping) & 0.045 & 0.249 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_oracle_rmse_rel]]\n148\tFrozen gated library & 0.126 & 0.701 & 0.074 & 0.839 & 0.379\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel]]\n149\tLinear exposure regression & 0.180 & 1 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_linear_rmse_rel]]\n150\tDifference-in-means & 0.622 & 3.450 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n151\tNeighbourhood HT & 0.994 & 5.510 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n152\t\\bottomrule\n153\t\\end{tabular}\n154\t\\end{table}\n155\t\n156\tTwo aspects need qualification. First, the gap to the linear baseline is 0.054 on a plane mean, % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#frozen_vs_linear_abs_gap]]\n157\t and the per-seed errors have a spread (Section~\\ref{sec:reliability}) that is comparable to a fraction of it; we did not run a paired test. Second, all estimators run on the same 24 instances per case, so the comparison is paired by instance, but the number of seeds is modest.\n158\t\n159\t\\subsection{Where the error concentrates: per-case contrasts}\n160\tFigure~\\ref{fig:main}b and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show that per-case error is bimodal across the eight cases. In the five cases whose generating form is a linear function of the one-hop neighbour fraction (cases 1--5), the frozen estimator's relative RMSE lies between 0.031 and 0.054. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#cases_1_to_5_range]]\n161\t In the three cases where the mechanism departs from that form it is 0.198 (saturating), 0.151 (count$\\times$degree) and 0.452 (two-hop). We read single-factor contrasts as follows, each from one case pair, with the caveat that no per-case interval exists:\n162\t\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{1pt}\n163\t\\item \\textbf{Assignment design} (case 1 vs.\\ 2, same graph and mechanism): 0.051 vs.\\ 0.031. Cluster assignment did not degrade accuracy; it also changes which exposure values are observed, so a difference of 0.020 should not be over-read.\n164\t\\item \\textbf{Graph family} (cases 1, 3, 4; same form): SBM 0.051, BA 0.054, WS 0.040. No degradation for the heavy-tailed BA graph or the near-regular WS graph.\n165\t\\item \\textbf{Interference strength} (case 1 vs.\\ 5): tripling $\\gamma$ from 1.5 to 4.5 gave 0.051 vs.\\ 0.034. Relative error did not grow with strength in this range; because the error is relative to a GATE that grows with $\\gamma$, a form-correct regression's relative error can shrink as the signal grows.\n166\t\\item \\textbf{Response shape} (case 1 vs.\\ 6): the saturating mapping raises error to 0.198. The pair also differs in $\\gamma$ (1.5 vs.\\ 2.5), so shape is not isolated.\n167\t\\item \\textbf{Degree dependence} (case 3 vs.\\ 7, both BA): 0.054 vs.\\ 0.151, a change in mechanism form on the same graph family.\n168\t\\item \\textbf{Spillover range} (case 1 vs.\\ 8): adding a two-hop term gives 0.452, the largest error of the grid and about 2.3 times that of the next worst case. The pair differs in total interference as well as range.\n169\t\\end{itemize}\n170\tThese contrasts are consistent with the hypothesis that the mismatch between the generating form and a fraction-linear mapping, rather than graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they do not support a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not establish it: the grid is not factorial, the three cases with higher error are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances, and we did not record which mapping the estimator selected, so a mapping mismatch is not shown for any case. Case 6 is a caution: the library contains its exact generating sigmoid, yet the error is 0.198, so a missing mapping alone cannot explain it; failure of the gate to select that mapping, averaging over several mappings, or finite-sample variance at the corners are all possible and untested. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, suggesting residual sampling noise at $n=3000$, though this compares a plane mean with per-case values.\n171\t\n172\t\\subsection{A heuristic account of the corner extrapolation}\\label{sec:corner}\n173\tThe pattern in Table~\\ref{tab:cases} can be read through a simple account that we offer as interpretation, not as a tested mechanism. Least squares fits the outcome surface where the data are: with Bernoulli assignment at $p=0.5$, a node of degree $d$ has neighbour fraction $f_i$ concentrated near 0.5 with spread of order $\\sqrt{0.25/d}$, so observed exposures cover the middle of $[0,1]$ much more densely than the corners $f=0$ and $f=1$ at which the GATE is evaluated. If $h$ is linear in $f$, the fitted slope is the same everywhere and the corner prediction is correct up to sampling error. If $h$ is nonlinear, the fitted slope is a local average of $h'$ over the observed range and the corner contrast $h(1)-h(0)$ is replaced by that slope times the corner distance, which is wrong by an amount that depends on how $h'$ differs between the middle and the ends of the range. For the sigmoid in case 6 (centre 0.5, steepness 10) the slope is largest at the middle, exactly where the data lie, so a linear fit would extrapolate a steep local slope to the corners; whether the gated estimator used a sigmoid or fell back to linear in this case is not known. For the two-hop case, an exposure mapping that omits the second-hop term loses a component of the effect that is entirely absent from $f$, while for count$\\times$degree interference the bias depends on how the count scales with degree across nodes. Under this account, graph family and assignment design matter only through how well the observed exposure distribution covers the corners and through noise; cluster assignment widens that coverage, a possible reason for the lower error in case 2. We did not measure corner coverage, so these statements remain hypotheses.\n174\t\n175\t\\subsection{Bias, variance and the model-selection comparison}\n176\tThe mean absolute relative bias on the final plane is 0.074, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n177\t which is 0.588 of the mean relative RMSE. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#bias_over_rmse]]\n178\t Per-case bias is not recorded, so a substantial systematic component exists on average but is not shown to be concentrated in the cases with higher error.\n179\t\n180\t\\begin{table}[t]\n181\t\\centering\\small\n182\t\\caption{Model-selection plane (8 seeds 101--108, same eight case specifications, different seeds): plane-mean relative RMSE for the three estimator classes, each scored once. These are selection-set numbers, not final evidence; per-case values, baselines, bias and coverage for this plane are not used here.}\n183\t\\label{tab:search}\n184\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n185\t\\toprule\n186\tEstimator class & Plane-mean rel.\\ RMSE\\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\tFlexible ridge ensemble & 0.232\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_001]]\n189\tLinear + design-weighted residual correction & 0.185\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_002]]\n190\tBIC-gated exposure library (selected) & 0.140\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_003]]\n191\t\\bottomrule\n192\t\\end{tabular}\n193\t\\end{table}\n194\t\n195\tTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. The gated library has the lowest plane-mean error (0.140), against 0.185 for the linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction and 0.232 for a flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging. Because the evaluator's per-case and baseline values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence we use, we draw no per-case or baseline-relative conclusion from it, and in particular we do not say here whether the two alternatives beat plain linear regression. The three classes differ in several respects at once (model family, selection rule, interval construction), so this comparison supports a ranking of the three configurations on these seeds and no attribution to any single component. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping, and the final-plane error in the two-hop case remains 0.452; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. The two-hop feature in the source counts length-two walks, including walks that return to the node, and so differs from the generating distance-exactly-two fraction; whether this matters, whether the gate ever selected it, and whether the all-treated corner is poorly constrained were not tested. No ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n196\t\n197\t\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\n198\t\\label{sec:reliability}\n199\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n200\t\\centering\n201\t\\includegraphics[width=0.72\\textwidth]{figures/reliability.pdf}\n202\t\\caption{(a) Per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases for the 24 final seeds, with the mean and a 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval for the mean (10{,}000 resamples over seeds). (b) Mean 95\\%-interval coverage over cases against the nominal level, with mean relative interval width.}\n203\t\\label{fig:rel}\n204\t\\end{figure}\n205\tPer-seed errors (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}a) have a mean of 0.102 with bootstrap interval [0.093, 0.113]; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_mean]]\n206\t this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8. Two seeds stand out (9001 and 9015, with per-seed errors 0.180 and 0.173), while every other seed lies between 0.078 and 0.119; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#outlier_seeds]]\n207\t excluding them the mean is 0.095. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#mean_excluding_outliers]]\n208\t Per-case decomposition for those seeds is not available, so we do not know which case drove them.\n209\t\n210\tThe nominal 95\\% intervals covered the truth at a rate of 0.839 averaged over cases, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_coverage]]\n211\t with mean relative width 0.379 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_width_rel]]\n212\t (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}b). This is undercoverage by 0.111 % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#coverage_shortfall]]\n213\t on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, treats units as independent in the sandwich estimate even under the cluster design, and uses no resampling (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, \\texttt{sandwich\\_se}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). Cluster-robust and network-aware variance estimation is a developed topic \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Han2023ModelbasedRA}; the interval here is not of that kind. Variance estimators for design-based effects, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment and random-graph asymptotics \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run any of them on this grid, so we cannot say whether they would restore nominal coverage. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortfall to a specific cause.\n214\t\n215\t\\subsection{Validity checks}\n216\tThe constant-outcome control returned estimates with maximum absolute value $9.0\\times10^{-15}$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\n217\t so the estimator returned an essentially zero estimate when the outcome carried no signal. With permuted outcomes the mean absolute estimate was 0.059 of the true GATE, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\n218\t and with a node-relabelled graph the relative RMSE was 0.627, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\n219\t which is similar to the difference-in-means value of 0.622 and much larger than the 0.126 headline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n220\t We do not interpret the similarity mechanistically. No control was vacuous, the replayed episode agreed exactly, and no episode failed. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\n221\t\n222\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n223\t\\label{sec:limits}\n224\t\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. A tentative practical reading is that effort spent on the exposure mapping, for instance by model comparison among a small family, is worth testing; this study does not isolate it. A gate of this kind also has costs we did not ablate: the choice of margin and library members is a source of researcher degrees of freedom.\n225\t\n226\t\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the variance of the former was prohibitive and the model risk of the latter was moderate to large only when the mapping was wrong. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size.\n227\t\n228\t\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Finally, relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it summarizes small effects and heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n229\t\n230\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic and known up to family; we ran no experiment on public graphs such as Facebook100 or ego-Facebook, none on real outcomes, and none with learned graph representations or designs other than the two above. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n231\t\n232\t\\section{Conclusion}\n233\tWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. Relative RMSE was 0.031--0.054 per case in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment, and 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle given the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping mismatch and extrapolation to the all-treated and all-control corners drive the larger errors; this account is consistent with the single-factor contrasts but untested, because the grid is not factorial and the selected mappings were not verified. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, case pairs without per-case intervals, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. The most direct next steps are a factorial extension over size, density and treatment probability with per-case intervals, an ablation of the gate and library with logged mapping choices, diagnostics for the two-hop failure, calibrated intervals (for example, cluster-aware resampling), and experiments on public graphs.\n234\t\n235\t% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n236\t% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n237\t\\clearpage\n238\t\\bibliography{references}\n239\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n240\t\n241\t\\clearpage\n242\t\\appendix\n243\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n244\t\n245\t\\subsection{Simulation Design and Case Specifications}\n246\tTable~\\ref{tab:spec} gives the parameters read from the case-specification file used on the final plane; model-selection seeds used the same eight specifications. Instances are generated from seeded random streams keyed by seed, graph family and node count. The evaluator requires $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and a 95\\% nominal interval level. Covariates are standard normal; in SBM cases the first covariate gets an additional block-level shift of scale 0.5.\n247\t\\begin{table}[h]\n248\t\\centering\\small\n249\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\\footnotesize\n250\t\\caption{Case specifications (all cases: $n=3000$, $\\sigma=1$, intercept 1, $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$).}\n251\t\\label{tab:spec}\n252\t\\begin{tabular}{clll}\n253\t\\toprule\n254\tCase & Graph parameters & Interference parameters & Design\\\\\n255\t\\midrule\n256\t1 & SBM, 10 blocks, deg$_{in}$ 14, deg$_{out}$ 6 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n257\t2 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster, $p=0.5$\\\\\n258\t3 & BA, $m=10$ & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n259\t4 & WS, $k=8$, rewire 0.1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n260\t5 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n261\t6 & same as 1 & sigmoid, $\\gamma=2.5$, steepness 10, centre 0.5 & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n262\t7 & BA, $m=10$ & count$\\times$degree, $\\kappa=0.15$, $m_{ref}=10$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n263\t8 & same as 1 & fraction + two-hop, $\\gamma=1.5$, $\\gamma_2=2.0$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\end{table}\n267\t\n268\t\\subsection{Estimator and Baseline Definitions}\n269\t\\paragraph{Feature maps of the library.} With $f$ the one-hop treated fraction, $s$ the treated-neighbour count, $f^{(2)}$ the two-hop walk fraction (length-two walks from the node that end at a treated node, divided by all length-two walks from the node, so walks returning to the node are included), $\\ell=\\log(1+d)$ centred, and $\\bar x$ centred covariates, the 15 mappings are: linear $[1,z,f,x]$; count $[1,z,s/\\bar d,x]$; two-hop $[1,z,f,f^{(2)},x]$; interactions $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,z\\bar x,f\\ell]$, $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,f\\ell]$ and $[1,z,f,x,f\\bar x]$; saturating $[1,z,1-e^{-kf},x]$; sigmoid $[1,z,(1+e^{-(f-t)/0.1})^{-1},x]$; and saturating-count $[1,z,1-e^{-s/(k\\,\\mathrm{med}_d)},x]$, where $\\mathrm{med}_d$ is the median degree over non-isolated nodes; the count mapping uses $s/\\bar d$ with $\\bar d$ the mean degree. Source: \\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py} (\\texttt{\\_exposure\\_states}, \\texttt{candidate\\_designs}, \\texttt{\\_fit\\_candidate}, \\texttt{bic\\_gate}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). All-treated values are the analytic corner values (for example $f=\\mathbf 1[d>0]$, $s=d$, $f^{(2)}=\\mathbf 1[\\text{walks}>0]$). The BIC is $n\\log(\\mathrm{RSS}/n)+p\\log n$ with $p$ the rank of the design. The sigmoid with $t=0.5$ and scale 0.1 equals the case-6 generating sigmoid up to the coefficient $\\gamma$, which the regression absorbs.\n270\t\n271\t\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n272\t\n273\t\\paragraph{Reference estimators.} DiM, linear and oracle regressions follow Section~3 (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py}); HT uses inclusion probabilities $p^{|N_i|}$ and $(1-p)^{|N_i|}$ for Bernoulli designs, with $|N_i|=d_i+1$, and a hypergeometric probability over blocks touched by the closed neighbourhood for the cluster design. The oracle features are $[1,z,x_1,x_2,zx_1]$ (covariates $x_1,x_2$) plus the true mapping ($f$; $f$ and $f^{(2)}$; the sigmoid with true centre and steepness; or $c\\sqrt{m_{ref}/d}$).\n274\t\n275\t\\subsection{Per-Seed Final Results}\n276\tTable~\\ref{tab:seeds} lists the 24 final seeds. The per-seed value is the mean relative error over the eight cases; runtime per seed (four estimate calls per case) ranged from 9.1 to 10.9 seconds, with median 10.4. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#median_duration_seconds]]\n277\t\\begin{table}[h]\n278\t\\centering\\small\n279\t\\caption{Final per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases (seeds 9001--9024).}\n280\t\\label{tab:seeds}\n281\t\\begin{tabular}{cccccccccccc}\n282\t\\toprule\n283\t9001&9002&9003&9004&9005&9006&9007&9008&9009&9010&9011&9012\\\\\n284\t0.180&0.096&0.091&0.116&0.089&0.113&0.090&0.088&0.090&0.091&0.094&0.080\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n285\t\\midrule\n286\t9013&9014&9015&9016&9017&9018&9019&9020&9021&9022&9023&9024\\\\\n287\t0.086&0.078&0.173&0.086&0.108&0.105&0.093&0.090&0.119&0.103&0.096&0.084\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n288\t\\bottomrule\n289\t\\end{tabular}\n290\t\\end{table}\n291\tThe mean is 0.102, the standard deviation 0.026 and the median 0.092. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_std]]\n292\t\n293\t\\subsection{Negative Controls and Determinism Checks}\n294\t\\begin{table}[h]\n295\t\\centering\\small\n296\t\\caption{Final-plane validity checks for the frozen estimator.}\n297\t\\label{tab:controls}\n298\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n299\t\\toprule\n300\tCheck & Value\\\\\n301\t\\midrule\n302\tConstant outcome, max $|$estimate$|$ & $9.0\\times10^{-15}$\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\n303\tPermuted outcomes, mean $|$estimate$|/|\\gate|$ & 0.059\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\n304\tNode-relabelled graph, relative RMSE & 0.627\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\n305\tVacuous controls & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_vacuity_violations]]\n306\tReplay absolute difference & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\n307\tMissing or failed episodes & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.missing_or_failed_episodes]]\n308\t\\bottomrule\n309\t\\end{tabular}\n310\t\\end{table}\n311\tThe constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control gives a mean absolute estimate of 0.059 times the true GATE, and the relabelled-graph error (0.627) is far above the headline error of 0.126 and similar to the difference-in-means value. We make no mechanistic claim from these values. None of these supports accuracy on its own.\n312\t\n313\t\\subsection{Model-Selection Comparison of Estimator Classes}\n314\tMain-text Table~\\ref{tab:search} reports the eight model-selection seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications. The three estimator classes were each scored once; the final estimator was frozen from this comparison, so these numbers are a selection set, not final evidence. Only the plane-mean scores are used in this paper; per-case, bias, coverage and baseline values for this plane are not reported. The two alternatives, as implemented in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_01/program.py} and \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_02/program.py}, are: (i) a flexible ensemble that fits ridge regressions on exposure features, compares candidates by cross-validation and a held-out extrapolation split, averages those within one standard error, and builds an interval from a design-resampling wild block bootstrap; and (ii) a linear-regression estimate plus a shrunk, degree-stratified HT-type residual correction with a design-based variance term. We did not inspect their per-case behaviour, so we offer no explanation for their scores.\n315\t\n316\t\\subsection{Reproducibility Notes}\n317\tThe directory \\texttt{source\\_evidence/} holds the three estimator source files (\\texttt{frozen\\_estimator}, \\texttt{estimator\\_01}, \\texttt{estimator\\_02}), the evaluator scripts for the two planes, both case-specification files and the evaluator's requirements file (numpy 2.2.6, scipy 1.15.3, scikit-learn 1.6.1, networkx 3.4.2). \\texttt{build\\_figures.py} reads the evidence registry and regenerates Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:rel} and the derived statistics (bootstrap with fixed seed 0). Table~\\ref{tab:cases} and Table~\\ref{tab:spec} come from the final case-specification file and the evaluator's per-case metrics; Table~\\ref{tab:seeds} comes from the per-seed evidence file; Table~\\ref{tab:search} uses the plane scores of the three model-selection runs. Each final seed took about ten seconds on CPU. The final evaluation used seeds 9001--9024; model selection used 101--108. The overview figure in the main text is a schematic and contains no measured values.\n318\t\n319\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n320\tLarge language models were used throughout this study. They proposed the three estimator classes compared on the model-selection seeds, wrote the estimator source code for them, produced the figures and analysis scripts, retrieved and screened the cited literature, and drafted this manuscript. All reported numbers come from a fixed evaluator code base run on the estimator source files, and from analysis scripts that read those outputs; no number was produced by a language model without a corresponding file. The estimator's per-case behaviour and mapping choices were not inspected beyond the recorded metrics, as noted in the limitations.\n321\t\n322\t\\end{document}\n323\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e77e47a8-1973-482c-8d1e-3bed91288163","uuid":"ee8dc074-b34d-4c5a-be00-e257d8f8b8f6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:49:10.966Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"\nTable~\\ref{tab:agg} lists the aggregate metrics.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final-plane aggregate metrics (24 seeds $\\times$ 8 cases). Bias is the mean over cases of the absolute mean signed relative error; coverage and width are for the nominal 95\\% interval and are available only for the studied estimator.}\n\\label{tab:agg}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nEstimator & Rel.\\ RMSE & Ratio to linear & Bias & Coverage & Rel.\\ width\\\\\n\\midrule\nOracle (true mapping) & 0.045 & 0.249 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_oracle_rmse_rel]]\nFrozen gated library & 0.126 & 0.701 & 0.074 & 0.839 & 0.379\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.rmse_rel]]\nLinear exposure regression & 0.180 & 1 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_linear_rmse_rel]]\nDifference-in-means & 0.622 & 3.450 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\nNeighbourhood HT & 0.994 & 5.510 & -- & -- & --\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTwo aspects need qualification. First, the gap to the linear baseline is 0.054 on a plane mean, % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#frozen_vs_linear_abs_gap]]\n and the per-seed errors have a spread (Section~\\ref{sec:reliability}) that is comparable to a fraction of it; we did not run a paired test. Second, all estimators run on the same 24 instances per case, so the comparison is paired by instance, but the number of seeds is modest.\n\n\\subsection{Where the error concentrates: per-case contrasts}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:main}b and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show that per-case error is bimodal across the eight cases. In the five cases whose generating form is a linear function of the one-hop neighbour fraction (cases 1--5), the frozen estimator's relative RMSE lies between 0.031 and 0.054. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#cases_1_to_5_range]]\n In the three cases where the mechanism departs from that form it is 0.198 (saturating), 0.151 (count$\\times$degree) and 0.452 (two-hop). We read single-factor contrasts as follows, each from one case pair, with the caveat that no per-case interval exists:\n\\begin{itemize}\\setlength{\\itemsep}{1pt}\n\\item \\textbf{Assignment design} (case 1 vs.\\ 2, same graph and mechanism): 0.051 vs.\\ 0.031. Cluster assignment did not degrade accuracy; it also changes which exposure values are observed, so a difference of 0.020 should not be over-read.\n\\item \\textbf{Graph family} (cases 1, 3, 4; same form): SBM 0.051, BA 0.054, WS 0.040. No degradation for the heavy-tailed BA graph or the near-regular WS graph.\n\\item \\textbf{Interference strength} (case 1 vs.\\ 5): tripling $\\gamma$ from 1.5 to 4.5 gave 0.051 vs.\\ 0.034. Relative error did not grow with strength in this range; because the error is relative to a GATE that grows with $\\gamma$, a form-correct regression's relative error can shrink as the signal grows.\n\\item \\textbf{Response shape} (case 1 vs.\\ 6): the saturating mapping raises error to 0.198. The pair also differs in $\\gamma$ (1.5 vs.\\ 2.5), so shape is not isolated.\n\\item \\textbf{Degree dependence} (case 3 vs.\\ 7, both BA): 0.054 vs.\\ 0.151, a change in mechanism form on the same graph family.\n\\item \\textbf{Spillover range} (case 1 vs.\\ 8): adding a two-hop term gives 0.452, the largest error of the grid and about 2.3 times that of the next worst case. The pair differs in total interference as well as range.\n\\end{itemize}\nThese contrasts are consistent with the hypothesis that the mismatch between the generating form and a fraction-linear mapping, rather than graph family, strength or assignment, is what moves the error, and they do not support a simple story in which heavier-tailed degree distributions or stronger spillovers alone degrade regression GATE estimation in this range. They do not establish it: the grid is not factorial, the three cases with higher error are also the three that depart from the linear fraction form, each pair changes more than one property at a time in at least two instances, and we did not record which mapping the estimator selected, so a mapping mismatch is not shown for any case. Case 6 is a caution: the library contains its exact generating sigmoid, yet the error is 0.198, so a missing mapping alone cannot explain it; failure of the gate to select that mapping, averaging over several mappings, or finite-sample variance at the corners are all possible and untested. The oracle's plane mean of 0.045 is similar to the per-case errors in cases 1--5, suggesting residual sampling noise at $n=3000$, though this compares a plane mean with per-case values.\n\n\\subsection{A heuristic account of the corner extrapolation}\\label{sec:corner}\nThe pattern in Table~\\ref{tab:cases} can be read through a simple account that we offer as interpretation, not as a tested mechanism. Least squares fits the outcome surface where the data are: with Bernoulli assignment at $p=0.5$, a node of degree $d$ has neighbour fraction $f_i$ concentrated near 0.5 with spread of order $\\sqrt{0.25/d}$, so observed exposures cover the middle of $[0,1]$ much more densely than the corners $f=0$ and $f=1$ at which the GATE is evaluated. If $h$ is linear in $f$, the fitted slope is the same everywhere and the corner prediction is correct up to sampling error. If $h$ is nonlinear, the fitted slope is a local average of $h'$ over the observed range and the corner contrast $h(1)-h(0)$ is replaced by that slope times the corner distance, which is wrong by an amount that depends on how $h'$ differs between the middle and the ends of the range. For the sigmoid in case 6 (centre 0.5, steepness 10) the slope is largest at the middle, exactly where the data lie, so a linear fit would extrapolate a steep local slope to the corners; whether the gated estimator used a sigmoid or fell back to linear in this case is not known. For the two-hop case, an exposure mapping that omits the second-hop term loses a component of the effect that is entirely absent from $f$, while for count$\\times$degree interference the bias depends on how the count scales with degree across nodes. Under this account, graph family and assignment design matter only through how well the observed exposure distribution covers the corners and through noise; cluster assignment widens that coverage, a possible reason for the lower error in case 2. We did not measure corner coverage, so these statements remain hypotheses.\n\n\\subsection{Bias, variance and the model-selection comparison}\nThe mean absolute relative bias on the final plane is 0.074, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.bias_rel_abs_mean]]\n which is 0.588 of the mean relative RMSE. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#bias_over_rmse]]\n Per-case bias is not recorded, so a substantial systematic component exists on average but is not shown to be concentrated in the cases with higher error.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Model-selection plane (8 seeds 101--108, same eight case specifications, different seeds): plane-mean relative RMSE for the three estimator classes, each scored once. These are selection-set numbers, not final evidence; per-case values, baselines, bias and coverage for this plane are not used here.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\nEstimator class & Plane-mean rel.\\ RMSE\\\\\n\\midrule\nFlexible ridge ensemble & 0.232\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_001]]\nLinear + design-weighted residual correction & 0.185\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_002]]\nBIC-gated exposure library (selected) & 0.140\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.improvement_003]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three estimator classes on the eight model-selection seeds. The gated library has the lowest plane-mean error (0.140), against 0.185 for the linear model with a design-weighted, shrunk corner-residual correction and 0.232 for a flexible ridge ensemble with extrapolation-aware averaging. Because the evaluator's per-case and baseline values for this plane are not part of the verified evidence we use, we draw no per-case or baseline-relative conclusion from it, and in particular we do not say here whether the two alternatives beat plain linear regression. The three classes differ in several respects at once (model family, selection rule, interval construction), so this comparison supports a ranking of the three configurations on these seeds and no attribution to any single component. The library did contain a two-hop walk-fraction mapping, and the final-plane error in the two-hop case remains 0.452; why it did not resolve case 8 is \\emph{unknown}. The two-hop feature in the source counts length-two walks, including walks that return to the node, and so differs from the generating distance-exactly-two fraction; whether this matters, whether the gate ever selected it, and whether the all-treated corner is poorly constrained were not tested. No ablation of the gate margin or library components was run.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\n\\label{sec:reliability}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.72\\textwidth]{figures/reliability.pdf}\n\\caption{(a) Per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases for the 24 final seeds, with the mean and a 95\\% percentile-bootstrap interval for the mean (10{,}000 resamples over seeds). (b) Mean 95\\%-interval coverage over cases against the nominal level, with mean relative interval width.}\n\\label{fig:rel}\n\\end{figure}\nPer-seed errors (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}a) have a mean of 0.102 with bootstrap interval [0.093, 0.113]; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_mean]]\n this is the mean absolute relative error over cases, which is below the RMSE aggregate of 0.126 because RMSE weights large errors such as those in case 8. Two seeds stand out (9001 and 9015, with per-seed errors 0.180 and 0.173), while every other seed lies between 0.078 and 0.119; % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#outlier_seeds]]\n excluding them the mean is 0.095. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#mean_excluding_outliers]]\n Per-case decomposition for those seeds is not available, so we do not know which case drove them.\n\nThe nominal 95\\% intervals covered the truth at a rate of 0.839 averaged over cases, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_coverage]]\n with mean relative width 0.379 % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.ci_width_rel]]\n (Figure~\\ref{fig:rel}b). This is undercoverage by 0.111 % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#coverage_shortfall]]\n on the plane mean. The interval is built from HC1 sandwich errors for a fixed library, widened by the departure from the linear estimate; it does not model selection uncertainty beyond that spread, treats units as independent in the sandwich estimate even under the cluster design, and uses no resampling (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py}, \\texttt{sandwich\\_se}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). Cluster-robust and network-aware variance estimation is a developed topic \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Han2023ModelbasedRA}; the interval here is not of that kind. Variance estimators for design-based effects, block-bootstrap intervals for regression adjustment and random-graph asymptotics \\citep{Li2022RandomGraph} address network dependence; we did not run any of them on this grid, so we cannot say whether they would restore nominal coverage. We report the undercoverage as an observed outcome; per-case coverage is not recorded, and we do not attribute the shortfall to a specific cause.\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks}\nThe constant-outcome control returned estimates with maximum absolute value $9.0\\times10^{-15}$, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\n so the estimator returned an essentially zero estimate when the outcome carried no signal. With permuted outcomes the mean absolute estimate was 0.059 of the true GATE, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\n and with a node-relabelled graph the relative RMSE was 0.627, % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\n which is similar to the difference-in-means value of 0.622 and much larger than the 0.126 headline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_dim_rmse_rel]]\n We do not interpret the similarity mechanistically. No control was vacuous, the replayed episode agreed exactly, and no episode failed. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. Because these are the tested regime, we do not extrapolate to other sizes, densities or mechanisms. A tentative practical reading is that effort spent on the exposure mapping, for instance by model comparison among a small family, is worth testing; this study does not isolate it. A gate of this kind also has costs we did not ablate: the choice of margin and library members is a source of researcher degrees of freedom.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based and unbiased for its exposure contrast when the exposure condition is well defined, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the variance of the former was prohibitive and the model risk of the latter was moderate to large only when the mapping was wrong. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so any gain over linear regression partly reflects a match between the library and the grid. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Finally, relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it summarizes small effects and heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was designed after seeing model-selection per-case errors; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic and known up to family; we ran no experiment on public graphs such as Facebook100 or ego-Facebook, none on real outcomes, and none with learned graph representations or designs other than the two above. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. Relative RMSE was 0.031--0.054 per case in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment, and 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle given the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping mismatch and extrapolation to the all-treated and all-control corners drive the larger errors; this account is consistent with the single-factor contrasts but untested, because the grid is not factorial and the selected mappings were not verified. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, case pairs without per-case intervals, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. The most direct next steps are a factorial extension over size, density and treatment probability with per-case intervals, an ablation of the gate and library with logged mapping choices, diagnostics for the two-hop failure, calibrated intervals (for example, cluster-aware resampling), and experiments on public graphs.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Design and Case Specifications}\nTable~\\ref{tab:spec} gives the parameters read from the case-specification file used on the final plane; model-selection seeds used the same eight specifications. Instances are generated from seeded random streams keyed by seed, graph family and node count. The evaluator requires $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and a 95\\% nominal interval level. Covariates are standard normal; in SBM cases the first covariate gets an additional block-level shift of scale 0.5.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\\footnotesize\n\\caption{Case specifications (all cases: $n=3000$, $\\sigma=1$, intercept 1, $\\beta=(0.8,-0.5)$, $\\tau_0=1$, $\\tau_1=0.5$).}\n\\label{tab:spec}\n\\begin{tabular}{clll}\n\\toprule\nCase & Graph parameters & Interference parameters & Design\\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & SBM, 10 blocks, deg$_{in}$ 14, deg$_{out}$ 6 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n2 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & cluster, $p=0.5$\\\\\n3 & BA, $m=10$ & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n4 & WS, $k=8$, rewire 0.1 & fraction, $\\gamma=1.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n5 & same as 1 & fraction, $\\gamma=4.5$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n6 & same as 1 & sigmoid, $\\gamma=2.5$, steepness 10, centre 0.5 & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n7 & BA, $m=10$ & count$\\times$degree, $\\kappa=0.15$, $m_{ref}=10$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n8 & same as 1 & fraction + two-hop, $\\gamma=1.5$, $\\gamma_2=2.0$ & Bernoulli, $p=0.5$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Estimator and Baseline Definitions}\n\\paragraph{Feature maps of the library.} With $f$ the one-hop treated fraction, $s$ the treated-neighbour count, $f^{(2)}$ the two-hop walk fraction (length-two walks from the node that end at a treated node, divided by all length-two walks from the node, so walks returning to the node are included), $\\ell=\\log(1+d)$ centred, and $\\bar x$ centred covariates, the 15 mappings are: linear $[1,z,f,x]$; count $[1,z,s/\\bar d,x]$; two-hop $[1,z,f,f^{(2)},x]$; interactions $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,z\\bar x,f\\ell]$, $[1,z,f,x,\\ell,z\\ell,f\\ell]$ and $[1,z,f,x,f\\bar x]$; saturating $[1,z,1-e^{-kf},x]$; sigmoid $[1,z,(1+e^{-(f-t)/0.1})^{-1},x]$; and saturating-count $[1,z,1-e^{-s/(k\\,\\mathrm{med}_d)},x]$, where $\\mathrm{med}_d$ is the median degree over non-isolated nodes; the count mapping uses $s/\\bar d$ with $\\bar d$ the mean degree. Source: \\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_estimator/program.py} (\\texttt{\\_exposure\\_states}, \\texttt{candidate\\_designs}, \\texttt{\\_fit\\_candidate}, \\texttt{bic\\_gate}, \\texttt{\\_estimate}). All-treated values are the analytic corner values (for example $f=\\mathbf 1[d>0]$, $s=d$, $f^{(2)}=\\mathbf 1[\\text{walks}>0]$). The BIC is $n\\log(\\mathrm{RSS}/n)+p\\log n$ with $p$ the rank of the design. The sigmoid with $t=0.5$ and scale 0.1 equals the case-6 generating sigmoid up to the coefficient $\\gamma$, which the regression absorbs.\n\n\\paragraph{Fallbacks.} If the linear fit fails or any step raises, the estimator returns linear OLS with an HC1 interval; a constant outcome returns the estimate 0 with a degenerate interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Reference estimators.} DiM, linear and oracle regressions follow Section~3 (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluator/evaluate.py}); HT uses inclusion probabilities $p^{|N_i|}$ and $(1-p)^{|N_i|}$ for Bernoulli designs, with $|N_i|=d_i+1$, and a hypergeometric probability over blocks touched by the closed neighbourhood for the cluster design. The oracle features are $[1,z,x_1,x_2,zx_1]$ (covariates $x_1,x_2$) plus the true mapping ($f$; $f$ and $f^{(2)}$; the sigmoid with true centre and steepness; or $c\\sqrt{m_{ref}/d}$).\n\n\\subsection{Per-Seed Final Results}\nTable~\\ref{tab:seeds} lists the 24 final seeds. The per-seed value is the mean relative error over the eight cases; runtime per seed (four estimate calls per case) ranged from 9.1 to 10.9 seconds, with median 10.4. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#median_duration_seconds]]\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final per-seed mean relative error over the eight cases (seeds 9001--9024).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccccccccccc}\n\\toprule\n9001&9002&9003&9004&9005&9006&9007&9008&9009&9010&9011&9012\\\\\n0.180&0.096&0.091&0.116&0.089&0.113&0.090&0.088&0.090&0.091&0.094&0.080\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\midrule\n9013&9014&9015&9016&9017&9018&9019&9020&9021&9022&9023&9024\\\\\n0.086&0.078&0.173&0.086&0.108&0.105&0.093&0.090&0.119&0.103&0.096&0.084\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe mean is 0.102, the standard deviation 0.026 and the median 0.092. % @evidence[[derived_stats.json#seed_std]]\n\n\\subsection{Negative Controls and Determinism Checks}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Final-plane validity checks for the frozen estimator.}\n\\label{tab:controls}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nCheck & Value\\\\\n\\midrule\nConstant outcome, max $|$estimate$|$ & $9.0\\times10^{-15}$\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_constant_max_abs_est]]\nPermuted outcomes, mean $|$estimate$|/|\\gate|$ & 0.059\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_outcome_mean_abs_rel_est]]\nNode-relabelled graph, relative RMSE & 0.627\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_permuted_graph_rmse_rel]]\nVacuous controls & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.control_vacuity_violations]]\nReplay absolute difference & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.replay_abs_diff]]\nMissing or failed episodes & 0\\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.missing_or_failed_episodes]]\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\nThe constant-outcome control rules out spurious effects from the covariate and exposure terms, the permuted-outcome control gives a mean absolute estimate of 0.059 times the true GATE, and the relabelled-graph error (0.627) is far above the headline error of 0.126 and similar to the difference-in-means value. We make no mechanistic claim from these values. None of these supports accuracy on its own.\n\n\\subsection{Model-Selection Comparison of Estimator Classes}\nMain-text Table~\\ref{tab:search} reports the eight model-selection seeds (101--108) on the same eight case specifications. The three estimator classes were each scored once; the final estimator was frozen from this comparison, so these numbers are a selection set, not final evidence. Only the plane-mean scores are used in this paper; per-case, bias, coverage and baseline values for this plane are not reported. The two alternatives, as implemented in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_01/program.py} and \\texttt{source\\_evidence/estimator\\_02/program.py}, are: (i) a flexible ensemble that fits ridge regressions on exposure features, compares candidates by cross-validation and a held-out extrapolation split, averages those within one standard error, and builds an interval from a design-resampling wild block bootstrap; and (ii) a linear-regression estimate plus a shrunk, degree-stratified HT-type residual correction with a design-based variance term. We did not inspect their per-case behaviour, so we offer no explanation for their scores.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility Notes}\nThe directory \\texttt{source\\_evidence/} holds the three estimator source files (\\texttt{frozen\\_estimator}, \\texttt{estimator\\_01}, \\texttt{estimator\\_02}), the evaluator scripts for the two planes, both case-specification files and the evaluator's requirements file (numpy 2.2.6, scipy 1.15.3, scikit-learn 1.6.1, networkx 3.4.2). \\texttt{build\\_figures.py} reads the evidence registry and regenerates Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and~\\ref{fig:rel} and the derived statistics (bootstrap with fixed seed 0). Table~\\ref{tab:cases} and Table~\\ref{tab:spec} come from the final case-specification file and the evaluator's per-case metrics; Table~\\ref{tab:seeds} comes from the per-seed evidence file; Table~\\ref{tab:search} uses the plane scores of the three model-selection runs. Each final seed took about ten seconds on CPU. The final evaluation used seeds 9001--9024; model selection used 101--108. The overview figure in the main text is a schematic and contains no measured values.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used throughout this study. They proposed the three estimator classes compared on the model-selection seeds, wrote the estimator source code for them, produced the figures and analysis scripts, retrieved and screened the cited literature, and drafted this manuscript. All reported numbers come from a fixed evaluator code base run on the estimator source files, and from analysis scripts that read those outputs; no number was produced by a language model without a corresponding file. The estimator's per-case behaviour and mapping choices were not inspected beyond the recorded metrics, as noted in the limitations.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":188,"startLine":136,"totalLines":323}}}
